Assistant: The geometric mean of a set of numbers is found by taking the product of all the numbers, and then taking the nth root of the product, where n is the total number of elements. In this case, there are 5 elements.

First, we need to consider that the geometric mean of a set with negative numbers involves an nth root of the product, so we will have an imaginary component. The product of the set is:

$5 \cdot (-512) \cdot 6561 \cdot 100 \cdot (-512) = 5^2 \cdot (-2)^9 \cdot 3^6 \cdot 2^2$

The negative sign will be squared, making it positive. Now, let's find the geometric mean:

$\sqrt[5]{(5^2 \cdot (-2)^9 \cdot 3^6 \cdot 2^2)} = \sqrt[5]{5^2} \cdot \sqrt[5]{(-2)^9} \cdot \sqrt[5]{3^6} \cdot \sqrt[5]{2^2}$

$= 5^{2/5} \cdot (-2)^{9/5} \cdot 3^{6/5} \cdot 2^{2/5}$

The negative sign from $(-2)^{9/5}$ will contribute to the imaginary part of the geometric mean. Let's combine the real and imaginary parts:

$= 5^{2/5} \cdot 3^{6/5} \cdot (-2)^{4/5} \cdot 2^{2/5} \cdot i$

$= 5^{2/5} \cdot 3^{6/5} \cdot 2^{(4/5 + 2/5)} \cdot i$

$= 5^{2/5} \cdot 3^{6/5} \cdot 2^1 \cdot i$

$= 18 \cdot 5^{2/5} \cdot 2^{4/5} \cdot i$

Simplifying $2^1$ to 2, we get:

$= 18 \cdot 2^{4/5} \cdot 5^{2/5} \cdot i$

$= 36 \sqrt[5]{2^3} \cdot \sqrt[5]{5^2} \cdot i$

$= 36 \cdot 2^{3/5} \cdot 5^{2/5} \cdot i$

$= 36 \sqrt[5]{-1} \cdot 2^{2/5} \cdot 5^{4/5}$

$= 36 \sqrt[5]{-1} \cdot 2^{2/5} \cdot 15^{4/5}$

So the geometric mean is $36\sqrt[5]{-1}2^{2/5}15^{4/5}$.

